Uncompensated Caregiver Burden - Adjustment Disorder - V61.8 (Problem related to caregiver-care receiver relationship)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Unpaid care exhaustion, Self-healthcare neglect, Caregiver isolation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-049
Reason for Visit: Acute physical collapse characterized by severe, unremitting back pain and profound physical exhaustion, rendering the caregiver unable to mobilize.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (caregiver) and marginalized family member (care receiver)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute onset of severe, incapacitating lumbar back pain and extreme lethargy in a primary caregiver, leaving a highly dependent family member without support. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was necessary to rule out an acute spinal cord compromise, administer immediate analgesia, and assess the immediate safety of the care receiver prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I heard a pop in my back when I was lifting them, and now I can't stand up, but there is literally no one else to take care of them.
History of Present Illness: The patient (caregiver) describes a multi-year history of severe physical and emotional exhaustion from providing continuous unpaid care to a marginalized family member. Today, while performing a routine physical transfer, the patient experienced an acute, severe flare of somatic symptoms, specifically severe lumbar back pain, resulting in immediate functional collapse. The patient exhibits profound unpaid care exhaustion, chronic self-healthcare neglect (having ignored warning signs of back strain for months), and intense financial strain from reduced working hours. They report profound caregiver isolation as caregiving consumes all available time, sleep deprivation from round-the-clock monitoring, feelings of deep guilt despite superhuman efforts, and resentment toward societal structures that provide no support.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound, unable to stand, walk, or perform caregiving duties due to severe, acute lumbar pain and spasm.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of immediate danger to both the patient and the highly dependent care receiver due to the sudden, total collapse of the sole support system.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient is lying flat on the floor, visibly grimacing, diaphoretic, and extremely anxious regarding the care receiver.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild tachycardia secondary to pain. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Musculoskeletal: Severe, bilateral paraspinal muscle spasm in the lumbar region. Extreme tenderness to palpation over L4-S1. No midline bony tenderness. Straight leg raise is negative bilaterally (no radicular pain down the leg). Neurological: Sensation intact in bilateral lower extremities. Motor strength is difficult to assess due to pain, but gross movement of toes and ankles is intact. Normal bowel/bladder function currently reported. The clinical exam highlights profound unpaid care exhaustion; the patient is physically depleted and exhibits severe self-healthcare neglect, admitting they have not seen their own doctor in over three years.
Environmental Observations: The care receiver is safely situated in a medical bed nearby, but the residence lacks mechanical transfer aids (hoists).
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications for the caregiver.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lumbar Strain / Spasm secondary to Uncompensated Caregiver Burden - Adjustment Disorder - V61.8 - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Disc Herniation with Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of saddle anesthesia, new onset urinary/fecal incontinence, or profound, bilateral, progressive lower extremity weakness, though a standard disc bulge without cord compression is possible.

Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's age/bone density history and the mechanism of injury being a lifting strain rather than a direct fall or trauma, with pain primarily muscular rather than midline bony.

Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stone) - Ruled out/unlikely as the pain is mechanical, exacerbated by movement, and bilateral across the lumbar muscles rather than colicky flank pain radiating to the groin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, functionally incapacitating severe lumbar muscle spasm and pain, directly resulting from the physical strain of uncompensated caregiving and profound physical exhaustion. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely essential to perform a neurological examination to definitively exclude a surgical spinal emergency (like Cauda Equina Syndrome), administer potent analgesia, and crucially, assess the immediate safety and care plan for the dependent care receiver. Delaying assessment would have left both individuals in an unsafe, unmanaged state, firmly justifying urgent, home-based medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly break the severe lumbar muscle spasm and control the acute pain. Enacted an emergency care plan, calling a local emergency respite service/extended family to temporarily take over care duties. Advised the usual GP to arrange for elevated allostatic load markers and psychometric confirmation of severe caregiver role strain on the Zarit Burden Interview.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute back injury safely, implement a "strict flat-rest protocol": lie completely flat on the floor or a firm mattress with knees bent over a pillow for exactly 48 hours, standing only for the bathroom; enforcing this rigid physical boundary is the only way to heal the muscular tear. To mitigate future self-healthcare neglect, implement a "concurrent appointment rule": schedule the caregiver's own preventative health checkups on the exact same day and same clinic as the care receiver's appointments; linking the tasks ensures the caregiver's health is no longer indefinitely postponed.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community social work for emergency respite care, home care packages, and physical therapy for the caregiver.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgent request for a mechanical hoist for future transfers.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to monitor the back injury, review neurological status, and expedite the social work and equipment referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if developing numbness around the groin/buttocks (saddle anesthesia), sudden loss of bowel or bladder control, or if the legs suddenly become too weak to bear weight.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic and require ongoing review.
Clinical Course: The acute muscle spasm and pain will slowly improve over 7 to 14 days with strict rest, anti-inflammatories, and muscle relaxants. Long-term functional recovery requires the immediate introduction of mechanical transfer aids.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you suddenly lose control of your bladder or bowels, if you develop numbness between your legs, or if you cannot feel your feet.

